---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProfileSchema from '../components/ProfileSchema.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../lib/links';
import '../styles/pages.css';
---
<Base
  title="About Greg Weinger"
  description="Greg Weinger — 25 years in leadership, and an introvert. Why he hosts The Introverted Leader and what he believes about leading with quiet strengths."
>
  <ProfileSchema slot="head" />

  <PageHero eyebrow="About" title="Greg Weinger" lead="25 years in leadership — and an introvert.">
    <img slot="aside" class="about-hero-portrait" src="/greg-weinger.webp" alt="Greg Weinger" width="220" height="220" />
  </PageHero>

  <section class="band band--white">
    <div class="band-inner band-read prose">
      <p class="lead">I spent 25 years in leadership — and I'm an introvert. For most of that time, I assumed those two things didn't go together.</p>

      <p>The companies I encountered seemed to reward the loudest voice, the fastest answer, and effortless charisma. They didn't seem built for the way I naturally work: thinking before speaking, going deep instead of wide, and leading through preparation and trust rather than volume. So I spent years trying to perform a version of leadership that wasn't mine.</p>

      <p>What I eventually learned is that the traits I'd treated as liabilities were where my best leadership came from — the listening, the writing, the thoughtful question that changes the conversation. I started <em>The Introverted Leader</em> to have the conversations I wish I'd had earlier in my career: with researchers, coaches, and leaders who've figured out how to rise without becoming someone else.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--light">
    <div class="band-inner band-read">
      <h2 class="eyebrow">What I believe</h2>
      <blockquote class="belief-card">
        <p>Quiet strengths are underrated, not second-rate. You don't have to become an extrovert to lead, be visible, or get promoted. You have to understand how your strengths create value — and make that value legible to the people who decide. <span class="hl">That's the whole project.</span></p>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--white">
    <div class="band-inner band-read prose">
      <h2 class="eyebrow">Background</h2>

      <p>I spent 25 years working in and around leadership — long enough to notice what actually helps people thrive when the loudest-voice-wins dynamic doesn't fit how they operate.</p>

      <p>Through <em>The Introverted Leader</em>, I explore the research, strategies, and stories I wish I'd had earlier: conversations with researchers who study how introverts process and lead, coaches who work with quiet professionals, and leaders who've learned how to rise without performing a version of themselves that isn't real.</p>

      <p>I write a companion newsletter where I share what I'm taking away from each guest — the one idea that stuck, the question it opened up, and what I'm applying. It goes out weekly alongside every new episode.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--light">
    <div class="band-inner band-read band-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Connect</p>
      <h2 class="band-title">Find me here.</h2>
      <div class="band-actions link-buttons">
        <a class="btn-gold" href={LINKS.newsletter}>Subscribe to the newsletter</a>
        <a class="btn-primary" href={LINKS.linkedin}>LinkedIn</a>
        <a class="btn-primary" href={LINKS.substack}>Substack</a>
        <a class="btn-primary" href={LINKS.apple}>Apple Podcasts</a>
        <a class="btn-primary" href={LINKS.spotify}>Spotify</a>
        <a class="btn-primary" href={LINKS.youtube}>YouTube</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
